#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084524 :tutorial/content "To determine the axis of symmetry of the parabola\n\n$$\ny=ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nwe can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this result in a separate lesson. For now, let's get some practice working with it.\n\nSuppose we want to find the axis of symmetry of the function\n\n$$\ny=3x^2-6x+1\n$$\n\nTo do this, we substitute $a=3$ and $b=-6$ into the above formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -\\frac{b}{2a} \\\\\n&= - ((-6))/(2(3)) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is the equation of the axis of symmetry.\n\n![](images/88/88ee917eb387ee60e67080785519ffe9cb4333adcd602a733659d0447c37f61a.png)\n\nThe vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so $x=1$ also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we can substitute $x=1$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 3x^{2} - 6x + 1 \\\\\n&= 3(1)^{2} - 6(1) + 1 \\\\\n&= 3 - 6 + 1 \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(1,-2)$.\n\n![](images/a3/a391338ffb2bddd2a58b836e328c574d348215c518b27d9886ca11a21674943a.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bfd0e6a4-644a-50b6-91df-852343ebd41e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9241 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
